<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 2px solid #1a5276;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Where are you from? – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our New Classmates</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Where are you from?</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify specific information in descriptions and narratives. Interactive: Respond appropriately to spoken questions in familiar settings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify specific details in conversations about classmates' backgrounds and school experiences (e.g., "I'm from Chiriqui, and my old school had a big garden." "In my previous school, classes started at 7 a.m."). Can recognize key information in discussions about school schedules and community activities (e.g., "In my new school, I study in the afternoon, but before I had morning classes." "My new school organizes a book fair every September where students can buy and exchange books."). Can respond to simple questions about classmates' previous schools and new routines (e.g., "Where did you go to school before?" "I studied in San Miguelito." "Do you like the afternoon schedule?" "Yes, because I have mornings free for doing homework.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b><br>This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge, and introduce key vocabulary and pronunciation for the listening focus.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a map of Panama and pointing to different provinces. The teacher asks students, "Where are you from?" and models an answer: "I'm from Panamá City. Where are you from, Juan?" (pointing to a student). The teacher encourages students to say their hometown or province. Then, the teacher shows pictures of two different schools (e.g., a small rural school and a large urban school) and asks, "What do you see? What was your previous school like?" focusing on words like `big`, `small`, `noisy`, `quiet`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words by saying them clearly and slowly, exaggerating the /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ sounds for plurals and third person singular verbs. For example: "Our new <b>classmates</b> /s/," "This school has many <b>friends</b> /z/," "The teacher <b>watches</b> /ɪz/ us." The teacher also models short phrases like "I'm from Chiriqui," and "My old school used to have a big garden." The teacher clearly explains words like `classmate`, `hometown`, `previous`, `new`, `used to` using visuals and context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using questions like:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'old school,' do I mean the school I'm in now or the school I was in before?" (Expected answer: Before.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a `classmate` a friend from another school or a student in our class?" (Expected answer: A student in our class.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'used to walk,' does it mean I walk now or I walked in the past?" (Expected answer: In the past.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Students are exposed to new language in a meaningful context, focusing on listening for gist and specific information related to the theme and scenario.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our New Classmates".</b> The teacher plays an audio recording of two new classmates, Ana and Carlos, introducing themselves and talking about their previous schools and their new school experiences. The dialogue includes target vocabulary: `classmate`, `hometown`, `previous school`, `new school`, `schedule`, `cafeteria`, `playground`, `used to` and `come from`. It also uses Present Simple, Past Simple, and basic comparatives ("My new school is bigger than my old one.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short, informal dialogue (approx. 1.5 minutes) between two adolescents introducing themselves and describing their previous and new school experiences. The register is informal but appropriate for school settings. The content is an oral narrative with descriptive elements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify specific information in descriptions and narratives. Pragmatic: Responding to questions about classmates and schools (e.g., "Where are you from?" "I'm from Santiago." "What was your previous school like?" "It was small but had a big library.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher asks, "What are Ana and Carlos talking about?" (Expected: Their schools, where they are from).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and decide if statements are True or False based on the audio. E.g., "Ana is from Chiriqui." "Carlos's old school had a big cafeteria." "They like their new school schedule."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Information matching):</b> Students listen to match specific details (e.g., Ana's hometown -> Santiago; Carlos's previous school feature -> big playground).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[12-15 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>This stage focuses on controlled and semi-controlled listening practice, reinforcing target grammar and vocabulary for accuracy.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details using Present Simple (descriptions of new school/routines), Past Simple (descriptions of previous school/routines using `used to`), comparatives (comparing `new` and `previous` school features). Focus on `classmate`, `school`, `hometown`, `community`, `schedule`, `cafeteria`, `playground`, `previous`, `new`, `used to`, `come from`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap Fill:</b> The teacher provides a transcript of the audio dialogue with 8-10 key vocabulary words (e.g., `hometown`, `previous`, `schedule`, `cafeteria`, `classmates`) and verb forms (e.g., `used to walk`, `comes from`) blanked out. Students listen to the audio again (or a specific part) and fill in the missing words. Teacher checks answers as a class, focusing on correct word choice and pronunciation of plural/third person endings (e.g., "My new school <b>has</b> /z/ a big cafeteria.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Listen and Choose:</b> The teacher prepares a short audio recording with new short descriptions from other hypothetical `classmates` (e.g., "Hi, I'm Sofia. I used to study in Penonomé. My old school was small, but it had a very `colorful` playground. Now I am in a `big` new school with many `friendly` teachers."). After each description, students listen and choose the correct option from two or three choices on a worksheet (e.g., "Sofia's old school was: a) big, b) small, c) modern." or "What did Sofia's old school have? a) a big library, b) a colorful playground, c) a noisy cafeteria."). The teacher emphasizes listening for specific details.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students receive a short audio clip (e.g., "My new classmate, Roberto, comes from Colón. He used to play soccer every day after school."). Student A listens to the clip and tells Student B one piece of information they heard. Student B listens to another clip and tells Student A. They then compare and confirm. Teacher monitors for accurate listening and use of target vocabulary.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Students apply their listening skills in a communicative task, using the target language in a realistic scenario for fluency and interaction.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our New Classmates".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Listening for Classmate Profiles.</b>
                The teacher prepares several short audio snippets (30-45 seconds each), with different "new classmates" describing themselves, their `hometown`, `previous school` (using `used to`), and one `new` school expectation or routine (e.g., "Hi, I'm Ricardo. I come from Las Tablas. My previous school used to have a very `big` `playground`. Now, I hope my `new` `classmates` are `friendly`!"). Each snippet will be a distinct "classmate profile."
                Students work individually with a worksheet containing a table: "New Classmate Profile." Columns: Name, Hometown, Previous School Feature, New School Expectation/Routine.
                The teacher plays each audio snippet once or twice. Students listen carefully to identify the specific information for each "new classmate" and fill in their profile table. This activity requires active listening for key details and differentiating information.
                After listening to all snippets, students orally share one interesting detail they heard about a "new classmate" with a partner, practicing reporting what they listened to (e.g., "I heard that Ricardo's old school used to have a big playground.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connects directly to Theme: "Where are you from?" and Scenario: "Our New Classmates".</b> The task directly addresses finding out about new classmates' backgrounds and school experiences through listening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion.</b> The primary focus is accurate receptive listening and then briefly sharing the listened information. The peer sharing encourages a small element of collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment checks students' listening comprehension and their ability to recall and report information. Self-assessment encourages reflection.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Classmate Profiles" task and collection of the completed worksheets. The teacher will specifically observe students' ability to identify specific details and keywords from the audio inputs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "New Classmate Profile" worksheets with the information they filled in from the listening activity in STAGE 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying specific information (hometown, previous school feature, new school expectation/routine) from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., `hometown`, `previous`, `new`, `used to`) in written responses (if applicable) or brief oral reporting.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in understanding simple narrative details.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I heard and understood today was: _______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It was (easy/difficult) to listen for the specific details because _______."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>This final stage encourages students and the teacher to reflect on the learning process and plan for future lessons.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to our new classmates' stories did you find most interesting?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you would like to ask a new classmate about their previous school?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this lesson, especially concerning listening comprehension and vocabulary introduction? Were students able to identify specific details in the audio? Were the CCQs effective? What adjustments are needed for the next lesson to further support students in using new vocabulary and grammar (Present/Past Simple, comparatives) when discussing classmates' backgrounds and school experiences? Perhaps more varied listening inputs or more direct pronunciation practice for challenging sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and focused on listening for information about `new classmates` and their `previous schools`. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will build on this vocabulary and context by having students read a short text or profiles about similar scenarios, further consolidating their understanding of classmates' backgrounds and school experiences through reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[3-5 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>